'''Cabu Verd''' ({{langx|mxi|کابو ورد|Kabu Verdi|green cape}}) is an insular region of [[Balisca]], located in the [[Aethiopian Ocean]], roughly at the midpoint between the continent of [[Abyala]] and [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. The archipelago forms the eastern extremity of the [[Northern Triangle]]. 

Its largest islands are [[Almudévar]], [[Jayyān]], and [[Ceclavín]] (''Siglabiyín''). Many of its minor islands and islets are close to the larger islands, including [[Favaritx]], [[Carñada]], and [[S'Espalmador]]. The islands have a {{W|Humid subtropical climate}}, and the four major islands are all popular tourist destinations. The islands' culture and cuisine are heavily influenced by [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]] and [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]] settlers, but have developed their own distinctive features as they became increasingly cosmopolitan. Major cities include [[Alhambra]], [[Mocejón]], and [[Faraján]].

The archipelago was uninhabited prior to their discovery by Volisan sailors in 1499. In 1505, [[Moro people|Moros]] established the [[Colony of Alcázar]] as the first permanent colony in Balisca. [[Slavery in Balisca|Slave labor]] fueled the growing {{W|coffee industry|coffee economy}}, but also fueled conflicts inside the colony, ultimately resulting in the [[Cabu Verdan Revolution]] and the alignment with the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Similarly to the other Maroon territories, the cabildo of Cabu Verd rejected the authority of the [[Restaurasionistas]], ultimately leading to a [[Maroon pirates|proliferation of piracy]] against the [[Volisanian Empire]].  

